L000354	\subsection{Extracting the gap from PQMC imaginary-time correlations}
L000355	
L000356	The PQMC simulations provide the imaginary-time correlation function
L000357	\begin{equation}
L000358	C(\tau)=\langle \hat{O}(0) \hat{O}(-\tau)\rangle\,,
L000359	\label{corr}
L000360	\end{equation}
L000361	where the brackets $\langle\;\rangle$ denote the Monte Carlo average defined in Eq.~\eqref{MCcorrelator}.
L000362	When the observable $\hat{O}$ is odd under parity symmetry, in the long imaginary-time limit $\tau\rightarrow \infty$ the correlation decays exponentially as
L000363	\begin{equation}
L000364	    C(\tau) \propto e^{-\Delta_o\tau},
L000365	\end{equation}
L000366	allowing us to extract the smallest odd gap $\Delta_o=E_{o,0}-E_0$ from an exponential fit at large $\tau$. The selection of the fitting window has to be automatized, as detailed below.
L000367	
L000368	It is worth noting that, by adopting the standard forward-walking approach~\cite{PhysRevB.52.3654}, the whole profile of the pure imaginary time correlation could be determined from
L000369	\begin{equation}
L000370	\langle\psi_0|\hat{O}(0)\hat{O}(-\tau)|\psi_0\rangle =
L000371	\langle \hat{O}(-\Delta\tau) \hat{O}(-\Delta\tau-\tau)\rangle\,,
L000372	\end{equation}
L000373	
L000374	where the time interval $\Delta\tau$ is chosen large enough to eliminate the dependence on $\psi_g(\mathbf{x})$, which would otherwise affect the behavior of the correlation function $C(\tau)$ for small values of $\tau$.
L000375	
L000376	With an even-parity operator $\hat{O}$, for which the ground-state expectation value is finite, the imaginary-time correlation function converges to a finite constant at large $\tau$.
L000377	
L000378	To isolate the exponential decay associated with the lowest even-parity excitation, we define the offset correlation
L000379	\begin{equation}
L000380	    \tilde{C}(\tau) = C(\tau) - \langle \hat{O}(-\tau_f)\rangle \langle \hat{O}(0)\rangle,
L000381	\end{equation}
L000382	where the imaginary time $\tau_f$ is chosen to be sufficiently long to reach the uncorrelated plateau regime.
L000383	The expectation values $\langle \hat{O}(-\tau_f)\rangle$ and $\langle \hat{O}(0)\rangle$ are independently evaluated in the PQMC simulation. The even gap $\Delta_e = E_{e,1}-E_0$ is hence extracted from an exponential fit
L000384	$\tilde{C}(\tau)\propto e^{-\Delta_e\tau}$ in the large $\tau$ regime
L000385	
L000386	
L000387	\emph{Choice of operators.}
L000388	For the odd gap we consider single-spin operators
L000389	$\hat{O}_i = \sigma_i^z.$
L000390	In an initial short stint of the simulation, all $N$ sites are considered, and the site index $i$ providing the largest signal at a suitably chosen imaginary time is selected on-the-fly for the remainder of the simulation.
L000391	For even parity, we consider two-site operators of the form
L000392	$\hat{O} = \sigma_i^z\sigma_j^z$.
L000393	Again, the optimal pair $i,j$ is selected on-the-fly from a batch of $10$ randomly chosen pairs after an initial short simulation stint.
L000394	Illustrative examples of the odd and even correlation functions for one random instance of the quantum SK model are shown in Fig.~\ref{figEM8}.
L000395	
L000396	\begin{figure}[h]
L000397	\centering
L000398	\includegraphics[width=1.\columnwidth]{figEM8.pdf}
L000399	\caption{
L000400	Odd and (offset) even imaginary-time correlation functions $C(\tau)$ and $\tilde{C}(\tau)$ for a random realization of the quantum SK model at transverse field $\Gamma=1.5$ and system size $N=100$.
L000401	The bold segment indicates the asymptotic exponential scaling from which the lowest odd $\Delta_o$ is extracted.
L000402	}
L000403	\label{figEM8}
L000404	\end{figure}
L000405	
L000406	\emph{Selection of the fitting window.}
L000407	The imaginary-time correlations (${C}(\tau)$ or $\tilde{C}(\tau)$ for odd or even operators, respectively) decay exponentially only after an initial transient at small $\tau$ in which higher excitations still contribute. On the other hand, at very long imaginary times the signal-to-noise ratio deteriorates, eventually making the  signal comparable with its statistical uncertainty $\sigma(\tau)$. To isolate the region dominated by a single exponential decay, we restrict the fitting window to $\tau\in [\tau_{\mathrm{min}}, \tau_{\mathrm{max}}]$. $\tau_{\mathrm{max}}$ is tuned so to exclude data where the signal-to-noise falls below a predefined threshold, typically $C(\tau)/\sigma(\tau)\approx 8$ ($\tilde{C}(\tau)$ is used for even gaps).
L000408	
L000409	To select the left extreme $\tau_{\mathrm{min}}$, we increase it until  the fitted gap converges (from above) to a plateau value. The plateau region signals that the single-exponential assumption is valid. In practice, the procedure stops when the fitted gap remains constant within statistical uncertainty for consecutive windows or  if it starts increasing.
