L000509	
L000510	Beyond the one-dimensional case, exact theoretical approaches are not available. Hence, we benchmark the PQMC algorithm against gaps numerically obtained via the exact eigenvalue solver described in the main text.
L000511	Specifically, we consider the two-dimensional Gaussian Edwards-Anderson model of size $N=L^2=25$ at the transverse field  $\Gamma=1.98$, corresponding to the estimate of the critical point $\Gamma=1.98(7)$~\cite{PhysRevE.110.065305}. Fig.~\ref{figSM2} shows the odd gaps for 11 realizations of the disorder. These gaps display large random fluctuations. Yet, the predictions of the PQMC algorithm match the exact results within the statistical uncertainties for all instances.
L000512	Notably, the PQMC results are independent of the number of hidden neurons $N_h$ included in the restricted Boltzmann machines. This finding numerically confirms the derivation provided in the main text, which asserts that the PQMC gap estimator is independent of the choice of the guiding wave function $\psi_g(\mathbf{x})$.
L000513	
L000514	To further assess the reliability of the PQMC gap estimator at scale, we analyze the odd gap distribution across a large ensemble of disorder realizations. The testbed model is, again, the 2D critical Gaussian EA Hamiltonian including $N=25$ spins. The comparison of the gap histogram against the corresponding eigenvalue-solver results -- shown in Fig.~\ref{figSM3} -- denotes, again, the fidelity of the PQMC estimator.
L000515	
L000516	The error bars of the PQMC histogram are determined by averaging over a large ensemble of randomized gap datasets with added Gaussian fluctuations scaled with the statistical uncertainty of the corresponding gap values.
